  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential hypertension, stage 2 (I10)</content>. Home average 144/90 confirms stage 2 hypertension. No target-organ damage on the ECG or fundi; kidney function normal. PREVENT 10-year cardiovascular risk about 6 percent and Pooled Cohort Equations about 8 percent. Under the 1844 guideline, stage 2 hypertension calls for medicine with lifestyle change, with a goal below 130/80. With Dr. Okafor-Lindqvist he chose amlodipine, which needs no laboratory monitoring and does not cause low sodium or kidney injury with the dehydration and cold diuresis of polar travel. A statin was discussed at a borderline risk; he chose lifestyle change for now. [S]<list><item>Amlodipine 5 mg every morning from today.</item><item>Home readings twice a day for a week before the four-week review, then weekly.</item><item>Echocardiogram for stage 2 hypertension.</item><item>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, missed at intake, at the next visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Obesity, class 1 (E66.811)</content>. He wants to lose about 5 percent of his weight before sailing, by walking, hill work and smaller portions; no weight-loss medicine before departure. [S]<list><item>His own plan, reviewed at each visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D insufficiency (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 22 ng/mL in midwinter, with years of polar darkness ahead. [S]<list><item>Vitamin D3 25 micrograms (1,000 units) daily from today and through the expedition; recheck before departure.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Expedition preparation (Z71.84)</content>. Dr. Okafor-Lindqvist finds no barrier to the command so far and advises cardiac testing, a nutrition and food-safety plan, cold-injury training, vaccinations and an evacuation plan. [S]<list><item>Exercise treadmill test in about four weeks.</item><item>Vaccinations and dental check at the preventive visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Office blood pressure (seated, five minutes&apos; rest, large cuff, average of two readings)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>148/92 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home blood pressure monitoring</td><td>At diagnosis and two to four weeks after each treatment change, then weekly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven-day average 144/90 mmHg confirms stage 2 hypertension; repeat a week before the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, BMI and waist</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>90.4 kg, BMI 31.3, waist 106 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Cardiac auscultation</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No murmur or gallop</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fundoscopic examination</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No hypertensive retinopathy</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Basic metabolic panel, potassium and eGFR</td><td>Yearly (every six months only on a diuretic or renin-angiotensin blocker)</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at intake: sodium 140, potassium 4.2, creatinine 0.98 mg/dL, eGFR 88</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: total cholesterol 188, HDL 52, LDL 112, triglycerides 120 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>HbA1c or fasting glucose</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>HbA1c 5.6% at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The urine sample planned at intake was not collected, and none was given today. Without it, early kidney damage cannot be excluded, but creatinine, eGFR and the fundi are normal, so no harm is likely in the next few weeks. Corrective action: a urine cup given today and a standing order for the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>1.9 mIU/L at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>12-lead ECG</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at intake; no left ventricular hypertrophy</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>At diagnosis of stage 2 hypertension or suspected left ventricular hypertrophy</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked before the four-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Renal ultrasound or renal artery imaging</td><td>Only for resistant hypertension or a suspected secondary cause</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No features of a secondary cause; potassium normal</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ankle-brachial index</td><td>Every two to three years from 65 or with a smoking history</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Age 58 and never smoked; foot pulses present</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cardiovascular risk (PREVENT and Pooled Cohort Equations)</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PREVENT about 6 percent; Pooled Cohort Equations about 8 percent; statin declined for now</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 depression screen</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 3 at intake, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Amlodipine safety: ankle swelling, dizziness, flushing</td><td>When started and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started today; told to report ankle swelling or dizziness</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Wilderness and expedition medicine consultation</td><td>Before clearance for remote duty</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Okafor-Lindqvist today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Exercise treadmill test before strenuous remote work</td><td>Before clearance</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked in four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nutrition and food safety for the expedition (vitamin C and D, rations, cans, lead, botulism)</td><td>Before departure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Vitamin D3 started; vitamin C plan and food-safety advice given</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Confirm or exclude hypertension with a week of home readings. Outcome by 1845-01-27: Home average 144/90 mmHg: stage 2 hypertension confirmed [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg at home before departure. In progress; target 1845-04-07 [S]</item><item>Lose about 5 percent of body weight before sailing by his own plan of walking, hill work and smaller portions. In progress; target 1845-05-05 [S]</item><item>Medical clearance for the command with a written expedition health plan, medical kit and evacuation plan. In progress; target 1845-05-05 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypertension and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (2024); Clinical Guidelines for Diagnosis and Treatment of Botulism, 2021 (2021); Recommendations for medical management of adult lead exposure (2007); Weight Loss to Prevent Obesity-Related Morbidity and Mortality in Adults: Behavioral Interventions (2018); Statin Use for the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease in Adults: Preventive Medication (2022)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Echocardiogram and urine sample before the four-week review. Home readings for a week before then. Call for chest pain, breathlessness, fainting or ankle swelling. Next scheduled visit 1845-02-24: four-week review after starting amlodipine: blood pressure, side effects, echocardiogram and urine albumin. [S]</paragraph></text>

